{% extends "specification_user.jinja2" %}
{% block requirements %}
Create the Processing Flow Specification in English. Use flow identifiers such as FLOW-001, preferring existing source identifiers. Reference the preceding six specifications to connect business initiation, completion, and recovery.

## Flow Inventory and Prerequisites
List flow IDs, purposes, users and systems, triggering events, prerequisites, completion conditions, and related specification IDs.
## Normal Processing
In execution order, describe actors, screen operations, API calls, inputs and outputs, applicable business rules, data reads and writes, state transitions, batch and asynchronous integrations, and transaction boundaries.
## Branches, Failures, and Recovery
Show conditional branches, alternative paths, mutual exclusion and parallel processing, exception IDs, retries, compensation, termination points, and resumption points.
## Flow Diagrams and Specification Mappings
Create Mermaid flowcharts or sequence diagrams for major flows supported by the source, matching step numbers and specification IDs in the text.
Provide a mapping table from screens to APIs, data, batch jobs, business rules, and exceptions. Do not invent elements to complete a diagram.
## Acceptance Criteria
Summarize validation conditions for representative normal, branching, failure, and recovery paths.
## Consistency and Open Questions
Summarize unresolved references, contradictions, and questions across all seven specifications. Do not silently change earlier specification bodies or identifiers.
{% endblock %}
